WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:05.341
Okay, let's spend some time today talking
about Medicare for all Medicare for all is

00:00:05.341 --> 00:00:06.640
becoming a bigger and bigger, bigger

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.250
part of the 2020 democratic primary. It rightly
so. I mean it makes sense. This is one of

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:14.600
the biggest issues. How are we going to pay
for healthcare? How are we going to organize

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:20.020
healthcare? And there are now lots of inaccurate
things that are being said about Medicare

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:26.770
for all proposals. And there is lots of inaccuracy
also about allegations about the positions

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:31.210
of different candidates around Medicare for
all. So what I want to do today, and I hope

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:35.030
that this will be useful and understood and
in the good faith that I'm presenting it,

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:40.960
I want to go through the bad arguments against
Medicare for all, but I also want to bring

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:48.690
up the more serious and sober critiques that
are the areas in which we should be focused

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:54.860
because these are the stronger criticisms
of the proposal. So to set the stage generally

00:00:54.860 --> 00:01:00.980
if you're already triggered, because I might
say something about Medicare for all is not

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:01.980
perfect.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.859
You've got to come back to the real world.
There is no political proposal that is perfect

00:01:05.859 --> 00:01:12.279
or without caveat or risks or liabilities.
This is the world we live in. Every everything

00:01:12.279 --> 00:01:18.590
has the other side of the ledger. Everything
is risk versus reward. Everything is opportunity

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:25.719
versus liability and what we want to figure
out is what proposal or plan is on balance

00:01:25.719 --> 00:01:30.119
the best and most efficient when it comes
to healthcare for delivering the best and

00:01:30.119 --> 00:01:36.909
highest quality and most available care at
the lowest cost. We shouldn't be afraid in

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:42.599
serious adult conversations about discussing
the pros and cons of any policy, even a policy

00:01:42.599 --> 00:01:47.130
that we think is the best one or better than
the status quo and in fact, I would make it

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:54.729
further argument. We want, we need to be prepared
for when people argue against Medicare for

00:01:54.729 --> 00:02:01.130
all, we need to be able to identify these
are the serious arguments to be discussed

00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:05.880
versus these are the silly, bad faith, ignorant
arguments that aren't really worthy of flow

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.020
for further exploration.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:12.640
My prediction is that most of my audience
understands why I'm doing this and will like

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.209
it and appreciate it, but I do know that there
are people who will attack me merely for talking

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:22.819
about here are question marks that remain
about Medicare for all, which completely misses

00:02:22.819 --> 00:02:29.620
the point. So first and foremost, bad critiques
of Medicare for all. There are many of these.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:36.709
One that comes up often is the countries that
have programs like Medicare for all have small

00:02:36.709 --> 00:02:42.370
populations. The U S is huge. Therefore, Medicare
for all wouldn't work in the United States.

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:47.329
This is a bad critique of Medicare for all.
Number one bigger population actually means

00:02:47.329 --> 00:02:53.459
that you're spreading the risk across more
people. There's no one small group or people

00:02:53.459 --> 00:02:58.440
with a particular condition that can throw
off the actuarial realities of what it costs

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:03.280
to cover people. You can make the opposite
case that actually the bigger the risk pool,

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:06.490
the safer and more efficient and better priced.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:09.621
Something like Medicare for all would be that.
That's a bad argument. Now. Secondly, you

00:03:09.621 --> 00:03:13.640
can also disprove that by counter example
because there are many very large countries

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.810
that have nationalized systems of different
kinds. Germany has 83 million people. Their

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:21.969
system is not Medicare for all, but it is
a system that is far better than the one we

00:03:21.969 --> 00:03:26.760
have and has elements of Medicare for all.
Japan has 130 million people. I think you

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:33.689
get the point. Now that being said, you can
make the case that what will make uh, the

00:03:33.689 --> 00:03:39.420
us implementation more of a struggle or a
challenge, uh, are the large rural areas of

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:44.329
the United States because large rural areas
can be more of a concern in terms of providing

00:03:44.329 --> 00:03:50.590
access to healthcare services. But that's
not an issue with Medicare for all specifically.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:54.620
That's an issue more broadly in a country
that has a lot of rural areas and it has nothing

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:56.480
to do with Medicare for all.

00:03:56.480 --> 00:04:02.040
Second bad argument that we hear about Medicare
for all Medicare for all will lead to long

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:07.889
waits for services and it'll lead to rationing
of care. And you know, death panels was one

00:04:07.889 --> 00:04:12.950
of the phrases that used to be thrown around.
We already have those things. There is a shortage

00:04:12.950 --> 00:04:17.700
of dermatologists in much of the U S you sometimes
have to wait three or four months as a new

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:22.310
patient to see a dermatologist in all, but
what would be considered like emergency, you

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:26.260
know you might have skin cancer. It needs
to be looked at. Right now there's already

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:32.930
rationing in our system because for profit
insurance companies are deciding who's covered

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:39.370
what is covered when and how much. You can
already see de facto rationing and it impacts

00:04:39.370 --> 00:04:46.169
how people obtain or avail themselves of care
that they would like to have. There will sometimes

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:50.930
be weights and there will sometimes be rationing
under Medicare for all.

00:04:50.930 --> 00:04:54.930
It will be different than under the current
system, but it's not unique to Medicare for

00:04:54.930 --> 00:05:00.750
all and it is not a unique criticism of Medicare
for all whatsoever. Then you get into the

00:05:00.750 --> 00:05:06.550
general, we can't afford it. That's a terrible
criticism because we can't afford our current

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:12.350
system. If the barometer for affordability
is people can get the care that would be medically

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:18.139
suggested at a price they can afford. We already
have a problem with affordability. Affording

00:05:18.139 --> 00:05:23.370
Medicare for all is a question of political
will. It's a question of deciding what kind

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:27.390
of a system are we going to have and deciding
to put in the means to pay for it. Now there

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:31.620
are also, we'll talk more about costs in a
moment. There are also just really dumb criticisms

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:36.250
of Medicare for all. Like it's communism.
These aren't remotely serious. I'm not even

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:39.199
going to waste your time with that today,
but just know that if that's, if you hear

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:43.060
those words in a critique of Medicare for
all, it's not a serious critique.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:49.690
So let's now get to what are the more important
and sensible critiques of Medicare for all.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:54.360
We all want to know what these are so that
we can be prepared. Uh, seriously, I hope

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.580
people understand what I'm doing here. I'm
not attacking Medicare for all, despite the

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:04.500
following critiques. It may still be the best
system, but this is how serious people evaluate

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:10.159
important proposals. We figure out what are
the strongest arguments against it and then

00:06:10.159 --> 00:06:15.840
can we still defend this as the best policy
in light of those arguments, does it stand

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:21.960
up to scrutiny as remaining the best plan?
First of all, just accepting that Medicare

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:27.410
for all isn't the only way to get to universal
coverage. We should all be doing. That's,

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:32.720
this isn't a criticism, okay? It's a reality.
Lots of countries have universal coverage

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.150
that people can afford without Medicare for
all or something that would be equivalent

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:40.449
in that country, but with other plans I've
talked about many of them.

00:06:40.449 --> 00:06:43.650
Medicare for all could still be the best.
It could still be the simplest, it could be

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:51.500
the cleanest or not, but it's important not
to say that Medicare for all is the only way

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:55.090
to achieve healthcare for everyone because
there are many examples from around the world

00:06:55.090 --> 00:07:02.280
that are different. Second, the idea that
costs will go down for everyone or almost

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:08.430
everyone. That is a possible truth. It is
possible that you can structure Medicare for

00:07:08.430 --> 00:07:15.440
all so that cost goes down for most people,
but if you look at serious analyses, you will

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:23.860
see that in order for costs to go down for
most people, you need either to justify drastically

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:28.530
reducing what healthcare costs per person
in the United States beyond just what the

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:33.509
bureaucracy adds to the cost. I'll get to
that momentarily or you need a more modest

00:07:33.509 --> 00:07:36.310
plan in terms of what's covered and I'll explain
that now.

00:07:36.310 --> 00:07:39.669
This is not my opinion. If you look at the
work of the urban Institute which is left

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:47.169
leaning, they say that to do exactly what
Bernie wants to do. Overall healthcare spending

00:07:47.169 --> 00:07:53.150
might actually go up. I'm just the messenger
here. If you do what Bernie wants to do in

00:07:53.150 --> 00:07:59.759
terms of taxation level, but with more modest
covered benefits, then the cost might go down.

00:07:59.759 --> 00:08:04.911
For most people now, this is not an attack.
Let's be adults. No system is perfect. There

00:08:04.911 --> 00:08:10.069
are many ways to set up a plan. There are
left leaning analyses that say it is not as

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:15.909
cut and dry as Bernie's numbers make it out
to be. Now that's okay. It might still be

00:08:15.909 --> 00:08:20.759
the best proposal we have and it would definitely
be better than what we have right now, but

00:08:20.759 --> 00:08:25.349
we're doing an analysis so let's stick with
this issue of cost for now.

00:08:25.349 --> 00:08:30.010
The argument is right now we have all these
different costs. We've got employee paid health

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:35.240
insurance premiums, employer contributions
to health care premiums. At many employers

00:08:35.240 --> 00:08:39.290
you've got your copays, some people have co-insurance,
you have an annual deductible you have to

00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:45.280
meet. There is a maximum yearly out of pocket.
Ah, it all adds up and the idea is Medicare

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:50.380
for all gets rid of all of that stuff and
it gets replaced with a payroll tax that is

00:08:50.380 --> 00:09:00.780
shared by employees and employers and overall
the cost comes down. This is in total a claim

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:05.220
that has some controversy around it. Even
when you look at nonpartisan and even left-leaning

00:09:05.220 --> 00:09:10.310
leaning economists, it's possible that that's
the way it would work. It's not guaranteed.

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:15.760
Okay. There are dense economic papers which
I encourage you to read from good economists.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:20.560
For example, Robert polling from the university
of Massachusetts who has consulted for Bernie

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:26.670
Sanders who says, the truth is we don't yet
have all the details to say for sure that

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.630
the middle-class is definitely going to save.

00:09:29.630 --> 00:09:36.170
Let me again be clear. Costs might go up slightly.
They would cover more so it might still be

00:09:36.170 --> 00:09:41.210
a trade we want to make and it still could
be the best proposal for most people, but

00:09:41.210 --> 00:09:47.030
serious economists say w it's a little early.
We don't really have the data to say that

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:51.570
a costs are going to go down for almost everyone.
The way that Bernie and Warren and others

00:09:51.570 --> 00:09:56.140
have been saying, it's not an attack or a
defense on any candidate. This is just the

00:09:56.140 --> 00:10:01.481
reality about these proposals. Let's do one
more thing related to the cost piece. Bernie

00:10:01.481 --> 00:10:08.450
has said you raise the money from an employee
tax and an employer tax. The employer tax

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:13.820
is only going to hit companies that have payrolls
of 2 million or more per year. If you look

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:21.380
at that, economists generally believe that
that tax, that the employers pay will be passed

00:10:21.380 --> 00:10:28.760
along to workers as either wage cuts or will
delay future wage increases or we'll lead

00:10:28.760 --> 00:10:31.650
to companies maybe not hiring as many people.

00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:36.660
That doesn't mean it's a bad plan. That's
something that economists say is not being

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:42.210
accounted for in the broader economic impact
of the proposals. That's a serious question.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:48.950
Now again, this might still be the best plan,
but we don't want to pretend that every question

00:10:48.950 --> 00:10:54.170
is answered by the proposals that we have
so far. Now there's one other detail. Uh,

00:10:54.170 --> 00:10:59.240
Bernie and Elizabeth Warren haven't made clear
who counts as middle-class. A big part of

00:10:59.240 --> 00:11:04.890
what they say is middle-class. Total costs
will go down. We need to know who counts as

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:08.740
middle-class in order to evaluate that. So
far we haven't gotten, that doesn't mean anything

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:12.571
is dishonest, doesn't mean anything is an
under the, you know, uh, untoward. But we

00:11:12.571 --> 00:11:20.061
need to know that next, aside from who pays,
there is the issue of total cost. And this

00:11:20.061 --> 00:11:26.750
is what I alluded to earlier. There are left
wing economists, serious economists who say

00:11:26.750 --> 00:11:33.100
proposals for Medicare for all are drastically
understating the cost to deliver care.

00:11:33.100 --> 00:11:37.440
Uh, one example is an economist named Kenneth
Thorpe. He was hired, remember when Vermont

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:41.530
was going to do state single-payer a, he's
not a right wing bomb thrower. He was hired

00:11:41.530 --> 00:11:45.910
by, by Vermont to help implement state single
payer, which ultimately didn't happen. Thorpe

00:11:45.910 --> 00:11:51.240
has said that as proposed, if we look at Bernie's
2016 Medicare for all plan, which is very

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:59.030
similar to the one we have today, uh, 71%
of households overall who currently have private

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:05.080
insurance might end up actually paying more
in total. When you account for the tax increases.

00:12:05.080 --> 00:12:09.930
Now they may have better access, they may
have more covered services, we still might

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:15.830
want to do it, but the claim that everybody's
costs will go down. This left wing economist

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:19.580
says there is a question Mark around that.
That's just one analysis, but it's an important

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:24.940
one and it's really important to understand
where the discrepancy comes from.

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:33.460
Thorpe says that Bernie's plan, the numbers
Bernie uses, they uh, change out when they

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:39.350
change out from a private insurance system
to a single payer system. They assume that

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:45.200
our per person healthcare spending will drop
to the healthcare spending per person in countries

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:50.390
like Canada and Australia. This is an assumption
that may never happen and it could take a

00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:56.570
long time. What I mean by that is Canada spends
48% less per person in the United States on

00:12:56.570 --> 00:13:03.040
healthcare. Australia spends 56% less per
person than in the United States. Part of

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:08.590
that discrepancy is the bureaucracy built
into the system that we have. And when you

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:12.950
get a more efficient system for paying like
Medicare for all, there will be a savings

00:13:12.950 --> 00:13:19.820
but there has not been a good answer to why
are we assuming total costs will go down to

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:23.060
what they are in, in uh, Australia and Canada.

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:27.200
Because there are many other reasons including
just how things are priced here in the United

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:31.780
States that will keep costs higher and that
throws the numbers of most of these Medicare

00:13:31.780 --> 00:13:37.930
for all proposals out of line. Now you can
make the case. Once it's a single payer system,

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:43.300
the government is going to be able to negotiate
costs of all services all the way down to

00:13:43.300 --> 00:13:49.320
what Canada and Australia have. That is a
bit optimistic by serious analyses. So like

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:54.591
that's a question Mark. So does this mean
Medicare for all is bad? No it doesn't. Does

00:13:54.591 --> 00:13:59.940
this mean Medicare for all is worse than what
we have now? Definitely not. It would be better.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:05.590
Does it mean we should abandoned Medicare
for all as a platform? Absolutely not. Especially

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:09.440
because remember a lot of Democrats already
oppose it and it's going to be a negotiation

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:15.420
no matter what happens in November of 2020
but we need to understand the details because

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:21.960
there are good faith questions about Medicare
for all that smart people on the left are

00:14:21.960 --> 00:14:29.090
bringing up and they're being brought up because
we want the best system, we want to anticipate

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:34.690
what arguments could be made against it and
we want to be able to distinguish bad arguments

00:14:34.690 --> 00:14:37.830
like the U S has too many people for this
to work.

00:14:37.830 --> 00:14:43.600
Terrible argument or it's communism, not even
an argument versus listen, if you look at

00:14:43.600 --> 00:14:49.730
the cost assumptions in most of these proposals,
they seem idealistic and potentially beyond

00:14:49.730 --> 00:14:55.330
what the facts support. That's the important
thing here and if we go through that, we might

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:59.760
still decide Medicare for all is the best
system or we might say Medicare for all is

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:04.640
a great system that has these problems and
here is another system, not the lame public

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:08.910
option, but serious other ways of getting
to universal coverage that have other issues,

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:13.290
but resolve some of the issues of Medicare
for all. This is how we have to think through

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:14.780
these serious problems

00:15:14.780 --> 00:15:19.420
and I think that's why it's worth taking the
time to go through it. Let me know what you

00:15:19.420 --> 00:15:20.071
think. I'm on Twitter at the Pacman. The show
is on Twitter at David Pakman show,

